messages:
alicia: ran an nsys sweep on the 1.8.3 regression, attaching timeline snippet. big perf hole around kernel-switching when kv-cache hits spike. wondering if our NVTX ranges are masking the real cost (we currently put ranges around 'kernel_exec' only)

alicia: example cmd i used: `nsys profile -o out_reg -t cuda,osrt --trace=nvtx,cuda --sample=none python run_trace.py`

alicia: timeline shows many tiny kernels (<200us) interspersed with a heavy `kv_lookup` phase; when batch size grows we see lots of kernel-selection overhead (kernel choose + small memcpy)

tyler: can you paste the NVTX range names you currently emit? iirc we do: `kernel_exec`, `batch_pack`, `kv_cache` and a large `request` range at the start

alicia: correct. our kernel selector does: NVTX range("kernel_exec") -> choose kernel -> launch -> NVTX pop. so all selector work is under kernel_exec which makes it hard to separate choose vs launch

chen: that explains it. selector + memcpy + small kernel launches get aggregated. suggestion: split into `k_sel`, `k_launch`, `k_exec` and keep per-request parent span like `rt/<reqid>` so you can filter by single request in perfetto

marco: caution: long NVTX names blow up the UI and add overhead. perf-bot: note from instrument-sanity-check: NVTX ranges with >64 chars start truncating in older nsight versions

infra-bot: perf-bot flagged that high cardinality parent spans can hurt aggregator. recommended: use short request id (8 hex chars) and optional `+detailed` toggle in env var

alicia: proposal: emit `rt/<shortid>` as parent (only when INSTRUMENT_NVTX_REQUESTS=1), then internal labels: `k_sel`, `k_pack`, `k_kv`, `k_launch`, `k_exec`. also add a guard to skip instrumentation for kernel durations <100us unless detail flag is set

tyler: i like the guard. instrumenting sub-100us kernels caused measurable overhead in our perf sandbox before

chen: for trace correlation add NVTX push/pop of numeric `req_tag` (int) in addition to string label. that makes filtering via regex optional and faster via ID

marco: are we comfortable adding extra memcpy between selector and launch? the selector currently does a small staging step that shows in traces as another kernel, maybe we can collapse the staging time into k_launch rather than new range

alicia: i'll experiment with both approaches: (A) split ranges but keep staging merged with launch; (B) strict split into sel/launch/exec and enable per-request parent for a small sample. i'll run nsys + perfetto and compare.

tyler: for the nsys runs, add `--stats=true` and upload `.nsys-rep`. also save the perfetto json for fast search. example: `nsys profile --stats=true -o out --trace=nvtx,cuda python perf_runner.py`

chen: quick code snippet for NVTX push/pop we discussed:

```cpp
if (INSTRUMENT_NVTX) {
  char buf[64];
  snprintf(buf, sizeof(buf), "rt/%08x", short_id);
  nvtxRangePushA(buf);
  // numeric tag
  nvtxMarkA((int)short_id);
}
// ... per-kernel
nvtxRangePushA("k_sel");
// selector work
nvtxRangePop();
nvtxRangePushA("k_launch");
// submit
nvtxRangePop();

if (INSTRUMENT_NVTX) nvtxRangePop(); // parent
```

marco: small nit: prefer `k:sel` style to make split on `:` easy in perfetto grouping tools

alicia: thanks all. i'll run both experiments and post a short summary + attached traces in this thread within the next 24h. leaning toward short names + request guard to keep traces usable in heavy-throughput tests :thumbsup:

perf-bot: automated test reminder: when you upload .nsys-rep, include git sha and runtime config to perf-archive
